As a rule, use of the "s"earch key to rest is
superior to use of the "." key. You find the most
unexpected stuff while just lazing around killing
time: the deadly trap just one square over, the door
in a direction you hadn't thought one could be, the
just arrived invisible monster, and such.

But to every rule, there is that deadly exception:
using "s" to rest in shops has gotten me killed too
many times to count.

Seems the one thing you'd really rather _not_ find
is a mimic that you already know is there, but don't
want to have know you know it is there.

In a jewelry shop, a drool producing stash of 36
rings, gems, or amulets, I was just following my
pooch around picking up everything he'd step on,
with intentions of naming them all "OK" after I had
the complete kit.  Once in a while I needed to stand
and wait for him to step on something else I could
grab. Two times too many, I used "s" instead of "."
to rest.

I was pretty much able to dodge and run around the
first mimic I alerted that way; mimics are _slow_.

It was the second mimic did for my archeologist that
was well on his way (all the way to Dlvl 3, in fact)
to the demigods' bar with a traveling salesman's
assortment of trinkets to sell.

Sigh.

Learn from my woeful tale: never, ever, start a game
of NetHack.

NetHack is more addictive even than tobacco.

All you will win is a memory of heartbreak and
constant cruelty on the part of the Dev Team, and
your opinion of your typing skills and general
intelligence are going to go right down the toilet.

xanthian, headed right back to be insulted again.

It's not like playing for a major sports franchise,
where there's a chance to win a championship ring
or similar trinket you'd get to _keep_, after all.
The stupid shopkeeper grabbed all those unpaid
treasures off my still warm corpse, so they didn't
even count toward my (pitiful) score.



